==English==
{{wp}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈtɛnd/}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈtɪnd/|aa=pin-pen}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-tend.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-tend.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛnd|s=1}}
* {{hyph|en|tend}}

===Etymology 1===
{{etymid|en|to incline towards}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*tend-|id=stretch}}
{{ety|en|:inh|enm:tenden}}
From {{inh|en|enm|tenden|id=to extend}}, from {{der|en|frm|tendre}}, from {{der|en|fro|tendre||to stretch, stretch out, hold forth, offer, tender}}, from {{der|en|la|tendere||to stretch, stretch out, extend, spread out}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|law}} To make a {{l|en|tender|id=offer}} of; to [[offer#Verb|offer]] or [[tender#Verb_3|tender]].
# {{lb|en|auxiliary}} To be [[likely]], or [[probable]] to do something, or to have a certain [[habit]] or [[leaning]]. {{defdate|from mid-14th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|They '''tend''' to go out on Saturdays.}}
#: {{ux|en|It '''tends''' to snow here in winter.}}
#: {{ux|en|I was '''tending''' to get up later and later after the breakup.}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1961
|first=Rhoads
|last=Murphey
|authorlink=Rhoads Murphey
|chapter=The Middle Kingdom: Splendid Isolation
|title=An Introduction to Geography
|series=Rand McNally Geography Series
|url=https://archive.org/details/introductiontoge0000murp/
|location=Chicago
|publisher=w:Rand McNally & Company
|LCCN=61-5047
|OCLC=491963
|page=418
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/introductiontoge0000murp/page/418/
|column=1
|text=Difficult broken country extends almost to the coast between Swatow (Shant’ou) on the south and Hangchow on the north, and this succession of barriers has '''tended''' to cut off southeast coastal China from the main body of the country.}} 
#* {{RQ:Austen Sense and Sensibility|volume=III|chapter=I|passage=She had found in her everything that could '''tend''' to make a farther connection between the families undesirable.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[contribute]] ''to'' or ''toward'' some outcome.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1812|author=William Cobbett|title=The Parliamentary History of England|passage=The Lords in 1722 declared that annexing such Clauses '''tends''' to the destruction of this government. And yet there are such bills every session and you pass them.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* In sense 2, this is a [[catenative]] verb that takes the ''to'' infinitive.
* See [[Appendix:English catenative verbs]]

